<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wodan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woden was the chief god of the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, corresponding to the Norse Odin and the continental Wodan. Associated with wisdom, war, poetry and the dead, he gave his name to Wednesday. Most Anglo-Saxon royal genealogies, including those of Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, Mercia and Lindsey, traced their kings' descent from him, and his name survives in place names such as Wednesbury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woden.jpg" alt="Woden" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg von Rosen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_von_Rosen_-_Oden_som_vandringsman,_1886_(Odin,_the_Wanderer).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Old English Wōden is a cognate of the Old Norse Óðinn, Old Saxon Wōdan and Old High German Wuotan. All derive from the reconstructed Proto-Germanic Wōðanaz, translated as &quot;lord of frenzy&quot; or &quot;leader of the possessed&quot;, from an adjective meaning &quot;possessed, inspired, delirious, raging&quot;. The root points to divine possession or inspiration and ecstatic divination. In the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> explicitly linked Wodan with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> furor, writing Wodan id est furor.</p><p>The Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, in his late 1st-century work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, recorded that among the gods the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> chiefly worshipped Mercury, to whom they offered human as well as other sacrificial victims. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/interpretatio-romana">interpretatio romana</a> this is generally taken to refer to Odin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a> as &quot;Hercules&quot; and Týr as &quot;Mars&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Old English royal genealogies record Woden as an ancestor of the kings of Lindsey, Mercia, Deira and Bernicia, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and East Anglia, in seven of the eight genealogies. Only Essex traced its ancestry instead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxnot">Saxnot</a>. Some genealogies give his father as Frealaf from the 8th century onward. The Welsh Historia Brittonum of the 9th century also places Woden in the pedigree of Hengist, with the ancestry &quot;filii Frealaf, filii Fredulf, filii Finn, filii Fodepald, filii Geta&quot;, Geta being said to be the son of a god other than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>. Other pedigrees reach further back through Geat, Beaw and Sceldi to Sceaf, who is the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</p><p>In such Old English texts the god is treated as a human ancestor of royalty, an approach scholars call euhemerism. Elsewhere in the Germanic world Odin was held to rule war and the dead, and he is depicted wielding a spear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The modern English weekday Wednesday derives from Old English Wōdnesdæg, &quot;day of Wōden&quot;. Cognates occur in Middle Low German and Middle Dutch Wōdensdach, modern Dutch woensdag, Old Frisian Wērnisdei and Old Norse Óðinsdagr, from which come Danish, Norwegian and Swedish onsdag. All derive from Late Proto-Germanic Wodanesdag, a calque of the Latin Mercurii dies, &quot;day of Mercury&quot;, seen in Italian mercoledì and French mercredi.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>, in his opera cycle Der Ring des Nibelungen, called the god Wotan, a spelling of his own invention that combines the Old High German Wuotan with the Low German Wodan. Most forms of the modern religious movement Heathenry venerate him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion was pagan: a Saxon baptismal vow of the late 8th or 9th century makes converts renounce Thunaer, <b>Woden</b> and Saxnot, and sacred sites included springs, groves and the Irminsul, a great pillar or tree near the Eresburg, destroyed by Charlemagne in 772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the early centuries CE, the Roman historian Tacitus described the Germanic peoples worshipping gods he identified with Mercury, Hercules and Mars, probably <b>Wodan</b>, Thor and Tiw, and the earth goddess Nerthus, whose image was carried in a wagon; they worshipped, he wrote, in sacred groves rather than temples.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A list of its kings survives in a collection of royal genealogies, tracing them to <b>Woden</b>, ending with Aldfrith in the late 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lindsey">Kingdom of Lindsey</a></li></ul></section>
